    <div class="entry-content" set:html={`<p data-start="93" data-end="344">Today, Let&#8217;s focus on the recently popular &#8220;superfood&#8221; flaxseeds and address the most common concerns people have, which is &#8220;Is Flaxseed Gluten Free?&#8221; and more things you should know.</p><p data-start="93" data-end="344"><img src="/images/dinweys-brown-flaxseed-300x300.jpg" decoding="async" class="alignnone wp-image-466" alt="dinweys brown flaxseed" width="494" height="494" title="Is Flaxseed Gluten Free? The Details You Must Know 3" /></p><div id="ez-toc-container" class="ez-toc-v2_0_85 counter-hierarchy ez-toc-counter ez-toc-grey ez-toc-container-direction"><div class="ez-toc-title-container"><p class="ez-toc-title" style="cursor:inherit">Table of Contents</p>
<span class="ez-toc-title-toggle"><a href="#" class="ez-toc-pull-right ez-toc-btn ez-toc-btn-xs ez-toc-btn-default ez-toc-toggle" aria-label="Toggle Table of Content"><span class="ez-toc-js-icon-con"><span class=""><span class="eztoc-hide" style="display:none;">Toggle</span><span class="ez-toc-icon-toggle-span"><svg style="fill: #999;color:#999" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="list-377408" width="20px" height="20px" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><path d="M6 6H4v2h2V6zm14 0H8v2h12V6zM4 11h2v2H4v-2zm16 0H8v2h12v-2zM4 16h2v2H4v-2zm16 0H8v2h12v-2z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg><svg style="fill: #999;color:#999" class="arrow-unsorted-368013" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="10px" height="10px" viewBox="0 0 24 24" version="1.2" baseProfile="tiny"><path d="M18.2 9.3l-6.2-6.3-6.2 6.3c-.2.2-.3.4-.3.7s.1.5.3.7c.2.2.4.3.7.3h11c.3 0 .5-.1.7-.3.2-.2.3-.5.3-.7s-.1-.5-.3-.7zM5.8 14.7l6.2 6.3 6.2-6.3c.2-.2.3-.5.3-.7s-.1-.5-.3-.7c-.2-.2-.4-.3-.7-.3h-11c-.3 0-.5.1-.7.3-.2.2-.3.5-.3.7s.1.5.3.7z"/></svg></span></span></span></a></span></div><nav><ul class='ez-toc-list ez-toc-list-level-1 ' ><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-1" href="#1_The_Truth_About_Flaxseeds_and_Gluten" >1. The Truth About Flaxseeds and Gluten</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-2" href="#2_Gluten_Hiding_Spots" >2. Gluten Hiding Spots</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-3" href="#3_The_Science_Behind_Gluten_Warnings" >3. The Science Behind Gluten Warnings</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-4" href="#4_Flaxseed_Consumption_Guide" >4. Flaxseed Consumption Guide</a></li></ul></nav></div><h2 data-start="346" data-end="777"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="1_The_Truth_About_Flaxseeds_and_Gluten"></span><strong data-start="346" data-end="389">1. The Truth About Flaxseeds and Gluten</strong><span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p data-start="346" data-end="777">According to the FDA’s gluten-free food standards (&lt;20ppm), flaxseeds are naturally gluten-free seeds [1]. This is due to their botanical properties—flax belongs to the flax family, while gluten proteins are only found in the endosperm of grasses such as wheat, barley, and rye. Just like a coconut tree doesn’t bear apples, flaxseeds and gluten are from entirely different “families.”</p><h2 data-start="779" data-end="1170"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="2_Gluten_Hiding_Spots"></span><strong data-start="779" data-end="805">2. Gluten Hiding Spots</strong><span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p data-start="779" data-end="1170">Pay special attention to these &#8220;major gluten sources&#8221; in your diet:<br data-start="875" data-end="878" />• <strong data-start="880" data-end="899">Obvious sources</strong>: Traditional pasta (one bowl of noodles contains about 10g of gluten), beer (each bottle contains 3-5g of barley gluten), instant oatmeal<br data-start="1037" data-end="1040" />• <strong data-start="1042" data-end="1060">Hidden sources</strong>: Soy sauce (15ml contains 0.5g of wheat protein), seasonings, and binders in processed foods like meatballs</p><h2 data-start="1172" data-end="1625"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="3_The_Science_Behind_Gluten_Warnings"></span><strong data-start="1172" data-end="1213">3. The Science Behind Gluten Warnings</strong><span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p data-start="1172" data-end="1625">Globally, 1% of the population suffers from celiac disease [2], and consuming 50mg of gluten (about 1/100th of a slice of bread) can cause intestinal damage. I’ve seen cases where fitness enthusiasts experienced persistent diarrhea for six months, only to be diagnosed with gluten sensitivity. Even in healthy individuals, excessive gluten intake can lead to bloating, fatigue, and other &#8220;brain fog&#8221; symptoms.</p><h2 data-start="1627" data-end="1729"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="4_Flaxseed_Consumption_Guide"></span><strong data-start="1627" data-end="1660">4. Flaxseed Consumption Guide</strong><span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p data-start="1627" data-end="1729">While flaxseeds are safe, there are some points to keep in mind:</p><ul data-start="1731" data-end="2605"><li data-start="1731" data-end="1994"><p data-start="1733" data-end="1994">Choose GFCO-certified products (the world’s strictest gluten-free certification) to avoid cross-contamination during processing. For instance, a certain oatmeal brand once had flaxseed products testing positive for gluten due to mixing on the production line.</p></li><li data-start="1996" data-end="2204"><p data-start="1998" data-end="2204"><strong data-start="1998" data-end="2030">Grind fresh for best results</strong>: Whole flaxseeds are only 35% digestible, but when ground, they can reach 95% digestibility [3]. I like to grind them fresh and sprinkle them on Greek yogurt at breakfast.</p></li><li data-start="2206" data-end="2411"><p data-start="2208" data-end="2411"><strong data-start="2208" data-end="2230">Control the dosage</strong>: Health Canada recommends no more than 45g per day [4], as excessive consumption can cause bloating. If you’re new to flaxseeds, start with 5g and monitor how your body responds.</p></li><li data-start="2413" data-end="2605"><p data-start="2415" data-end="2605"><strong data-start="2415" data-end="2449">Seal and store away from light</strong>: Flaxseeds are rich in Omega-3 (22.8g per 100g), so once opened, store them in the fridge and use them within a month to prevent oxidation and rancidity.</p></li></ul><p data-start="2607" data-end="2912">Since I started using ground flaxseeds instead of breadcrumbs for my chicken breast coating, not only have I solved the gluten issue, but my Omega-3 intake has also tripled. However, remember that even the best foods need to be paired scientifically. A healthy diet is always about variety and moderation.</p><p data-start="2914" data-end="3102" data-is-only-node="" data-is-last-node=""><strong data-start="2914" data-end="2929">References:</strong><br data-start="2929" data-end="2932" />[1] FDA Gluten-Free Labeling Final Rule<br data-start="2971" data-end="2974" />[2] Celiac Disease Foundation 2020<br data-start="3008" data-end="3011" />[3] Canadian Nutrition Society 2018 Research Report<br data-start="3062" data-end="3065" />[4] Health Canada Flaxseed Guidelines</p>`} />
